+## Radicle/src/nif/lint_checks.cpp:finite_position — non-finite positions are skipped by the sphere checks
+
+A half-float Inf or NaN position reaches the reader as a float Inf or NaN (`Radicle/src/nif/geometry.cpp:half_to_float`). NaN never equals the zero sphere, so `bounding_sphere_misses` reported such a shape as having no sphere, and an Inf position pushed the refit bounds to infinity. `finite_position` lets `bounding_sphere_misses` and both loops of `Radicle/src/nif/lint_fixes.cpp:refit_bounding_sphere` skip any vertex with a non-finite component. A shape whose positions are all non-finite is therefore neither judged nor refit, and a refit sphere is built from finite positions only. Residual: finite positions near the float maximum can still overflow the centre sum or a squared distance; vanilla positions are far below that.
+
+## Radicle/src/nif/skin_checks.cpp:weight_fault — a non-finite weight is named before the sign and sum tests
+
+The finite test comes first because NaN fails both later comparisons: `NaN < 0` is false and `fabs(NaN - 1) > tolerance` is false, so without the guard a NaN weight passes silently. An Inf weight would be reported as a sum fault instead. `Radicle/tests/test_nif_skin_checks.cpp:NonFiniteWeightIsNamedInsteadOfPassingTheSum` pins the NaN case.
+## Radicle/src/nif/lint_checks.cpp:repeated_vertex_fault — only a triangle that names one vertex twice is a verify failure
+
+The first draft judged zero-area triangles by an exact zero cross product. The real-data sample of 6,110 vanilla NIFs flagged 35 of them, carrying 1,850 collinear or coincident triangles whose three vertex indices are distinct (for example `Meshes\Architecture\Buildings\HouseKit\HouseKGrndAddonGarage01.nif`, block 14, which has eight). Vanilla ships those, so the check judges only `repeats_a_vertex`, a triangle whose index list repeats, which has zero area by construction. Across the full census of 226,454 vanilla NIFs, run once with the sampling stride set to 1, no shape repeats a vertex and no lint check fires. `Radicle/tests/test_nif_lint.cpp:CollinearTriangleWithDistinctVerticesIsNotReported` pins the narrowing.
+
+Only `BSTriShape` and `BSDynamicTriShape` are judged and repaired (`Radicle/src/nif/lint_checks.cpp:repairs_triangles`). A `BSSubIndexTriShape` carries a segment table that indexes its triangles, and a `BSMeshLODTriShape` carries LOD size fields after its triangles; the fix rewrites neither, so both are left out of the check as well as the repair.
+
+## Radicle/src/nif/lint_checks.cpp:sidecar_fault — a missing sidecar passes, only an unreadable one fails
+
+The `SSF File` of a `BSSubIndexTriShape` is a `SizedString16` after its segment table and, when `Num Segments` is below `Total Segments`, after the shared segment data (`Radicle/src/nif/lint_checks.cpp:segment_sidecar`, following `nif.xml:BSSubIndexTriShape`, `BSGeometrySegmentSharedData` and `BSGeometryPerSegmentSharedData`). The layout is confirmed on vanilla: in the 6,110-NIF sample every such block's string ends exactly at the block end (419 blocks).
+
+Vanilla names sidecars it does not ship. The full census made 7,478 lookups and 6,078 of them found no file (FaceGen heads and DLC robot and armour parts among them), and the game tolerates it, so absence cannot be a verify failure. The probe therefore fails only a file that exists beside the NIF and cannot be opened: the native host (`PluginEditTool/native/src/assets/NifRender.cpp:verifyReport`) and the real-data test both do that. The leaf name is what gets looked up, because the owner's checker in the NifSkope port also looks beside the mesh by file name.
+
+## Radicle/src/nif/lint_checks.cpp:unused_string_fault — a string counts as used on any four-byte match
+
+The reader has no schema for every field that holds a string index, so the check does not map references. A string is used when any four bytes of any block equal its index. That over-counts use: an unrelated integer or a float bit pattern keeps a truly unused string. The real-data sample found no unused non-empty string, and empty strings are not reported. `Radicle/tests/test_nif_lint.cpp:HeaderStringNoBlockNamesIsReported` pins the detection.
+
+There is no repair. Removing a string renumbers every index above it, and the over-count cannot tell a reference from a coincident number, so a byte-level renumbering would rewrite data that is not a reference.
+
+## Radicle/src/nif/lint_fixes.cpp:drop_repeated_vertex_triangles — the fix rebuilds only the triangle block
+
+The triangle count and data size are rewritten in the ten bytes before the vertex data. The vertex data and any bytes after the triangles (a `BSDynamicTriShape` keeps its dynamic vertices there) are copied unchanged. A shape whose every triangle repeats a vertex keeps them all, because an empty shape fails `shapes have vertices and triangles`. `Radicle/src/nif/fix_nif.cpp:require_same_shapes` compares the written triangles with `triangles_after_fix`, so any other change to the triangle list fails the fix instead of being written. A second fix finds no repeats and writes the same bytes (`Radicle/tests/test_nif_lint.cpp:RepeatedVertexTriangleIsDroppedAndTheFixIsIdempotent`).
+
+## PluginEditTool/native/src/assets/NifRender.cpp:verifyReport — the verify reply is JSON, with the old text inside it
+
+`NifService::verify` returns `{"text": ..., "checks": [{name, failure, fixable}]}`, where `text` is exactly the output the reply used to be. Fix and import replies still append that text form. The probe binds the NIF's folder and passes a missing file. The `nif_verify` MCP tool (`PluginEditTool/native/apps/fo4ide/McpTools.cpp`) forwards the reply, so its output is now the JSON string and the old text is in its `text` field.
+
+## PluginEditTool/web/src/nifmodes/Issues.tsx:Issues — every fix button runs the whole-file route
+
+The Fix route takes an input path and an output path and no check list, so each fix button, including a group's own, repairs every fixable finding in the file, and the confirm text says so. A group button appears only where `fixable` is true. Overwriting the open NIF asks through `useDialogs` with `danger` set; Save As picks a path with `BrowseForSave` and writes the fixed copy there, as `PluginEditTool/web/src/NifEditor.tsx:NifEditor` does. After a write `PluginEditTool/web/src/NifPanel.tsx:NifPanel` verifies the file it wrote.
+
 ## PluginEditTool/native/src/app/SwfTargetFile.cpp:gameArchives (game archive source for the SWF browse)
 
 `gameArchives` builds a read-only `fo4::archive::AssetResolver` over the game's BA2 archives, so the SWF panel can list and open archive entries. The Data folder is `roots[0]` of the `SwfRootsProvider`, because `swfRootsFor` puts `settings.dataFolder` first, and only that folder is read. The plugin names are the `.esm`, `.esp` and `.esl` files in it, sorted by name, passed to `fo4::archive::archive_load_order` with an empty INI list. This is the call `PluginEditTool/native/src/plugin/RadiclePluginStrings.cpp:RadiclePluginBackend::stringAssets` makes. `archive_load_order` then mounts `<plugin>.ba2` and `<plugin> - *.ba2` for each plugin, so `Fallout4 - Interface.ba2` is found through `Fallout4.esm`. The resolver is opened with no loose roots, so `AssetResolver::list` and `locate_in_archives` see archive entries only.
